---
/**
 * #1079 privacy controls (specs/analytics-privacy.md § UI Hooks, rows
 * "Controls" and "GPC state"). Rendered on /privacy/ only.
 *
 * The status line is a polite live region that states the current state in
 * plain words; controls.js fills it from window.npPrivacy and keeps it
 * current. Opting out and re-enabling are each one button (PRIV-14). While
 * Global Privacy Control is active the section carries data-np-privacy-gpc,
 * the grant button is disabled, and the explanation below it is shown
 * (PRIV-10).
 *
 * What the control covers and does not cover comes from
 * docs/privacy/inventory.md § Summary and § Capture Conflicts C5 (Cloudflare
 * Web Analytics is outside the opt-out), § Hosting and CDN, and § Other Third
 * Parties. Inline style and script for the reason given in PrivacyNotice.astro.
 */
import controlsSource from './controls.js?raw';
---

<style is:inline>
  .privacy-controls {
    padding: clamp(1rem, 2.5vw, 1.5rem);
    border: 1px solid var(--ink);
    background: var(--paper);
  }
  .privacy-controls__status {
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .privacy-controls__gpc {
    margin-top: var(--sp-md);
  }
  .privacy-controls__actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin-top: var(--sp-lg);
  }
  /* The status and actions ship hidden until controls.js runs; this keeps
     the display rules above from overriding the hidden attribute. */
  .privacy-controls [hidden] {
    display: none;
  }
  .privacy-controls .nav-button {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    line-height: normal;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .privacy-controls .nav-button:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid currentColor;
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }
  .privacy-controls .nav-button:disabled {
    cursor: not-allowed;
    opacity: 0.45;
    transform: none;
    background: transparent;
    color: inherit;
  }
  .privacy-controls__covers {
    margin-top: var(--sp-lg);
  }
  .privacy-controls__noscript {
    margin-top: var(--sp-md);
  }
</style>

<section
  id="np-privacy-controls"
  class="privacy-controls"
  data-np-privacy-ui="controls"
  aria-labelledby="np-privacy-controls-heading"
>
  <h2 id="np-privacy-controls-heading">Your choices</h2>
  <p class="privacy-controls__status" data-np-privacy-ui="status" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true" hidden>
    Reading your current setting.
  </p>
  <p id="np-privacy-gpc-explanation" class="privacy-controls__gpc" data-np-privacy-ui="gpc-explanation" hidden>
    While your browser sends Global Privacy Control, analytics cannot be turned on here. You can still turn them off; once your browser saves that choice, it keeps them off if your browser stops sending the signal.
  </p>
  <div class="privacy-controls__actions" hidden>
    <button type="button" class="nav-button" data-np-privacy-action="deny">Turn off analytics</button>
    <button type="button" class="nav-button" data-np-privacy-action="grant">Turn on analytics</button>
  </div>
  <noscript>
    <p class="privacy-controls__noscript">These controls need JavaScript. Without it, PostHog and Google Analytics do not load either.</p>
  </noscript>
  <p class="privacy-controls__covers">
    One setting covers both tools: PostHog, including session replay, and Google Analytics 4. It does not reach data already sent, Cloudflare's edge-injected web analytics and error reporting, the servers that serve the site, or the other services named below. Turning analytics off takes effect at once; turning them back on takes effect on the next page you load, provided your browser saves the choice.
  </p>
</section>

<script is:inline set:html={controlsSource} />
